If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient and the patient has an overdose-history then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient and the patient has a substance-use-disorder-history then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient and the patient has a sleep-disordered-breathing then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient and the patient has an opioid-dosage and the opioid-dosage includes at least 50 MME-units-per-day then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient and the patient takes a benzodiazepine then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician prescribes an opioid to a patient and the patient has a lost-tolerance-high-dose-return-risk then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
